fix(servers): a sign-in sheet opened by a refusal says which one, and never opens over a server that asks nothing

Two holes on the place path into the sheet.

**The refusal never arrived.** `SignInSheetRequest.refusal` exists, the sheet seeds its first round from it, and SMB's
side passes it, but `openSignInForPlace` forwarded only `firstOutcome` as `hostKey` and dropped everything else. So a
person sent to the sheet by `authentication_rejected` or `needs_credentials` got an empty password box and no sentence
saying why they were being asked, which is the exact failure the refusal-under-the-field design exists to prevent. It is
now read off the dial through `server-outcomes.ts::readConnectOutcome`, the one reader, so the two keep their own
sentences: "that password didn't work" for one that was offered and refused, "Cmdr needs a password" for one that was
never offered. A REGISTERED volume in `needs_sign_in` had no dial to read, so `connect-flow.ts`'s arm 2 says
`needs_credentials` itself through the seam's new `refusal` field.

**`nothing` could open the sheet.** Both `servers/DETAILS.md` and `SignInCredentialFields.svelte` state that a shape
asking for nothing never reaches the sheet, but only one caller enforced it. `openSignInForPlace` opened on whatever
`getVolumeSignInState` answered, and `nothing` fell through to the read-only-username plus password renderer: a password
box over a key-only or agent-only server, whose `reconnect_with_credentials` answers `NotSupported` every time. The
guard now lives at the seam, which answers `{ signedIn: false }` without opening anything, so the pane's `signed_out`
banner with no button stays up and a new caller inherits the rule instead of having to remember it.

Three cells: the refusal reaching the sheet for each of the two kinds, and the seam refusing to open over `nothing`.
